      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to determine the dimensions of Mateen's backyard and then calculate its area. We'll approach this problem step by step.\n\n### Step 1: Determine the dimensions of the backyard\nWe know that walking the length 25 times will cover 1000 meters. Let's denote the length of the backyard as \\( L \\). The perimeter \\( P \\) of the backyard is what satisfies the condition that walking the perimeter 10 times will also cover 1000 meters.\n\nThus, we can set up the following equations based on the given information:\n\n1. Walking the length 25 times:\n   \\[\n   25L = 1000 \\, \\text{m}\n   \\]\n\n2. Walking the perimeter 10 times:\n   \\[\n   10P = 1000 \\, \\text{m}\n   \\]\n\nFrom the first equation, we can solve for \\( L \\):\n\\[\nL = \\frac{1000}{25} = 40 \\, \\text{m}\n\\]\n\nFrom the second equation, we can solve for \\( P \\):\n\\[\nP = \\frac{1000}{10} = 100 \\, \\text{m}\n\\]\n\n### Step 2: Use the perimeter to find the width of the backyard\nThe formula for the perimeter of a rectangle is:\n\\[\nP = 2L + 2W\n\\]\nwhere \\( L \\) is the length and \\( W \\) is the width. Substituting the known values:\n\\[\n100 = 2L + 2W\n\\]\nSince we already know \\( L = 40 \\):\n\\[\n100 = 2(40) + 2W\n\\]\n\\[\n100 = 80 + 2W\n\\]\nSubtract 80 from both sides to isolate terms involving \\( W \\):\n\\[\n20 = 2W\n\\]\nDivide by 2:\n\\[\nW = 10 \\, \\text{m}\n\\]\n\n### Step 3: Calculate the area of the backyard\nThe area \\( A \\) of a rectangle is given by:\n\\[\nA = L \\times W\n\\]\nSubstituting the values we found:\n\\[\nA = 40 \\times 10 = 400 \\, \\text{m}^2\n\\]\n\nThus, the area of Mateen's backyard is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{400}\n\\]",
